ead direction, gaze direction, and facial expression.\n\nUse Image 3 only as an auxiliary skeleton reference to help accurately understand the body's joint structure and limb positioning.\n\n【POSE】\nReplace the person's pose in Image 1 with the exact pose shown in Image 2.\n\nAccurately match the body position, arm and leg placement, hand position, joint angles, body tilt, shoulder position, hip position, head position, and overall body gesture shown in Image 2.\n\nImage 3 is only used to assist with understanding the underlying skeletal structure and joint relationships. The final pose must visually match Image 2.\n\n【HEAD DIRECTION & FACIAL ORIENTATION】\nThe head direction and facial orientation must strictly follow Image 2.\n\nMatch the exact direction in which the head is turned or tilted, including:\n\n* facing directly toward the camera\n* looking to the left or right\n* looking upward or downward\n* three-quarter head orientation\n* profile orientation\n* head tilt\n* chin position\n\nDo not automatically make the person face the camera. If the face in Image 2 is turned away from the camera, reproduce that orientation.\n\nThe facial orientation must remain naturally connected to the neck and body, with anatomically correct head rotation and perspective.\n\n【GAZE DIRECTION】\nThe person's eye direction must also follow Image 2.\n\nMatch whether the subject is:\n\n* looking directly at the camera\n* looking to the left or right\n* looking upward or downward\n* looking away from the camera\n\nDo not force eye contact with the camera if Image 2 does not show eye contact.\n\n【FACIAL EXPRESSION】\nThe facial expression must strictly follow Image 2 while preserving the person's facial identity from Image 1.\n\nMatch the expression shown in Image 2, including:\n\n* neutral expression\n* slight smile\n* natural smile\n* serious expression\n* relaxed expression\n* surprised expression\n* subtle emotional expression\n* mouth shape\n* lip position\n* eyebrow position\n* eye openness\n* eyelid shape\n* cheek tension\n* overall facial muscle tension\n\nDo not simply copy the face from Image 2. Preserve the identity, facial structure, and recognizable features of Image 1, while reproducing the expression and emotional state shown in Image 2.\n\n【SHOT SCALE / FRAMING】\nThe shot scale must strictly follow Image 2. Do not preserve the original framing or shot scale from Image 1.\n\nMatch Image 2 in terms of:\n\n* camera distance\n* subject size within the frame\n* framing\n* cropping position\n* visible portion of the body\n* headroom\n* body placement within the frame\n\nIf Image 2 is an extreme close-up, output an extreme close-up.\nIf Image 2 is a close-up, output a close-up.\nIf Image 2 is a medium shot, output a medium shot.\nIf Image 2 is a half-body shot, output a half-body shot.\nIf Image 2 is a 7/8-body shot, output a 7/8-body shot.\nIf Image 2 is a full-body shot, output a full-body shot.\n\nThe person's scale and cropping within the frame must closely match Image 2.\n\n【CAMERA ANGLE / VIEWPOINT】\nThe camera angle and viewpoint must strictly follow Image 2. Do not retain the original camera angle from Image 1.\n\nAccurately match Image 2 in terms of:\n\n* camera viewing direction\n* front / side / rear / three-quarter view\n* left-right camera orientation\n* horizontal viewing angle\n* eye-level / high-angle / low-angle perspective\n* camera elevation\n* perspective relationship between the subject and camera\n* subject orientation relative to the camera\n\nIf Image 2 shows a frontal view, use a frontal view.\nIf Image 2 shows a side view, use the corresponding side view.\nIf Image 2 shows a rear view, use a rear view.\nIf Image 2 shows a three-quarter or angled view, precisely reproduce that viewpoint.\n\nThe body, head, and face must naturally adapt to the new camera viewpoint with physically correct perspective and anatomy.\n\n【REFERENCE RESPONSIBILITIES】\n\nImage 1 provides:\n\n* person identity\n* facial identity and facial features\n* hairstyle\n* skin tone\n* body proportions\n* clothing and clothing details\n* accessories\n* background environment\n\nImage 2 provides:\n\n* body pose\n* head direction\n* facial orientation\n* gaze direction\n* facial expression\n* shot scale\n* camera angle\n* camera direction\n* viewpoint\n* framing\n* subject size within the frame\n* cropping\n\nImage 3 provides:\n\n* auxiliary skeletal and joint structure reference\n\n【FINAL RESULT】\n\nCreate a photorealistic image combining the person and background from Image 1 with the exact pose, head direction, facial orientation, gaze direction, facial expression, shot scale, framing, and camera viewpoint from Image 2, using Image 3 only as skeletal guidance.\n\nThe final image should look as if the person from Image 1 was originally photographed in the exact pose, viewpoint, framing, head direction, gaze direction, and facial expression shown in Image 2.\n\nDo not alter the person's identity, facial structure, hairstyle, body proportions, clothing, accessories, or background.\n\nOnly change the pose, head direction, facial orientation, gaze direction, facial expression, camera viewpoint, shot scale, and framing according to Image 2, with only the natural anatomical and perspective changes required to make the transformation physically correct.\n\nPhotorealistic human photography, highly realistic skin texture, natural facial anatomy, accurate body proportions, physically correct perspective, high resolution, sharp details, soft and evenly distributed lighting, realistic shadows, authentic photographic appearance, minimal AI-generated look.\n"], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "text"}, "name": "text", "label": "text", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "text"}], "flags": {"collapsed": true}, "id": 93, "type": "JjkText", "properties": {"widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "JjkText"}, "order": 2}, {"mode": 0, "outputs": [{"name": "birefnet_model", "links": [68], "label": "birefnet_model", "type": 